WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:01.680
YURI GRINSHTEYN: We've seen
how to create service level

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:03.840
objectives for services
running on GKE.

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:05.960
But what if your
services aren't?

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:08.400
Today, I'm going to show you
how to create custom services

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:10.920
and define SLOs to
measure their reliability.

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:12.270
This is The Stack Doctor.

00:00:12.270 --> 00:00:15.574
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:20.970
As we covered in
our earlier episode,

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:23.100
services running on
GKE are automatically

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:25.050
discovered by SLO
monitoring, if you were

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:26.700
using the Istio service mesh.

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:29.430
If you're not, you can
easily select GKE entities

00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:31.260
and create services
against them.

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:33.200
If your services
aren't on GKE, you

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:36.290
need to define them
as custom services.

00:00:36.290 --> 00:00:37.750
As you can see,
you're not limited

00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:39.917
to just the out-of-the box
service types and service

00:00:39.917 --> 00:00:40.550
monitoring.

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:42.010
You can create custom services.

00:00:42.010 --> 00:00:43.290
You have two options.

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:46.340
You can use the API directly, or
you can use an automation tool

00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:47.422
like Terraform.

00:00:47.422 --> 00:00:49.130
Once the service is
created, you can then

00:00:49.130 --> 00:00:51.930
use the UI to create
SLOs against it.

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:54.060
Let's take a look at
the API method first.

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:56.610
To invoke the API, make a
POST request of the services

00:00:56.610 --> 00:00:58.920
endpoint within your project,
specifying the service

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:00.232
ID as a parameter.

00:01:00.232 --> 00:01:02.190
The body of the request
should contain the name

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:03.920
you'd like to see in the UI.

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:05.010
And there you are.

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:06.690
Now that the service
has been created,

00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:09.480
you can use the UI to
create an SLO against it.

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:11.880
Note that you have to
specify "other" as the metric

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:12.552
for your SLI.

00:01:12.552 --> 00:01:14.010
This is a custom
service, and there

00:01:14.010 --> 00:01:16.290
is no automatic measure of
performance or availability

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:16.920
for it.

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:19.728
As such, you need to have
a good SLI for the service.

00:01:19.728 --> 00:01:22.020
For a start, you might be
able to use a metric admitted

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:24.210
by the Google Cloud load
balancer, such as request

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:25.290
count.

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:26.880
Otherwise, you
want to instrument

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:28.590
your service to admit SLIs.

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:30.850
For an example, take a
look at our earlier episode

00:01:30.850 --> 00:01:33.692
by using OpenTelemetry
to do just that.

00:01:33.692 --> 00:01:35.400
As you can see, creating
a custom service

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:37.740
using an API call is
pretty straightforward.

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:39.750
From there, you could
use the API or the UI

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:40.983
to define your SLO.

00:01:40.983 --> 00:01:42.900
But what if you want to
automate this process?

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:44.923
This is where
Terraform comes in.

00:01:44.923 --> 00:01:47.340
You may remember that we covered
using Terraform to create

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:49.290
monitoring configurations
like uptime checks,

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:50.832
dashboards, and the
learning policies

00:01:50.832 --> 00:01:51.960
in the previous episode.

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:53.040
If not, no worries.

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:54.810
We'll link to that
episode below.

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:56.460
To quickly bring
you up to speed,

00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:58.050
Terraform is an open
source tool that

00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:00.180
allows us to define
infrastructure and monitoring

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:01.860
configurations as code.

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:05.600
That includes defining
services and SLOs.

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:08.000
Here's how you can define a
service and an SLO against it

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:09.050
in Terraform.

00:02:09.050 --> 00:02:12.340
Let's walk through the code
together so that it's clear.

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:14.020
First, define the basics.

00:02:14.020 --> 00:02:15.670
Specify Google Cloud
as the provider,

00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:17.230
and define your service.

00:02:17.230 --> 00:02:19.390
For the service, you just
need to provide a service

00:02:19.390 --> 00:02:23.060
ID that's unique to your
project and a display name.

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:24.390
Next, define the SLO.

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:26.740
Specify an ID for it
and a display name.

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:29.320
Next, specify whether you
want to use a request-based

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:31.270
or a windows-based SLI.

00:02:31.270 --> 00:02:34.540
Then, specify the metric
you want to use as your SLI.

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:38.680
And finally, determine how you'd
like the SLI to be evaluated.

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:41.430
Note that I'm just using a
single metric in this example.

00:02:41.430 --> 00:02:43.895
For a request-based SLO, you
might have separate metrics

00:02:43.895 --> 00:02:45.270
that you want to
use to represent

00:02:45.270 --> 00:02:48.210
total requests and good requests
or perhaps the same metrics

00:02:48.210 --> 00:02:49.747
with different filters applied.

00:02:49.747 --> 00:02:51.330
Both of those are
absolutely supported

00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:54.890
by SLO monitoring to allow
you to calculate a ratio.

00:02:54.890 --> 00:02:57.530
The final step is to define
your target or goal, expressed

00:02:57.530 --> 00:02:59.750
as a fraction that
should be less than 1,

00:02:59.750 --> 00:03:02.270
and your compliance period as
either a rolling or a calendar

00:03:02.270 --> 00:03:03.470
window.

00:03:03.470 --> 00:03:06.170
Once your definition is done,
run the "terraform plan"

00:03:06.170 --> 00:03:08.270
command to validate
your configuration

00:03:08.270 --> 00:03:10.520
and make sure that the two
resources will be created--

00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:12.950
your service and your SLO.

00:03:12.950 --> 00:03:15.770
Finally, run "terraform
apply" to actually create

00:03:15.770 --> 00:03:16.910
the resources.

00:03:16.910 --> 00:03:18.950
Confirm that you want to
do this at the prompt,

00:03:18.950 --> 00:03:21.620
and verify that the two
resources you wanted got

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:23.710
created.

00:03:23.710 --> 00:03:25.630
Return to the
console, and verify

00:03:25.630 --> 00:03:27.460
that your service was
created and that it

00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:29.470
has an SLO defined against it.

00:03:29.470 --> 00:03:31.480
In the services detail
screen, make sure

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:34.090
that your service is currently
compliant with your SLO,

00:03:34.090 --> 00:03:36.130
if that's what you expect.

00:03:36.130 --> 00:03:37.760
Thanks for joining me today.

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:40.240
We learned how to define
custom services using the API

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:42.550
and Terraform and how
to automate setting up

00:03:42.550 --> 00:03:44.260
SLOs against our services.

00:03:44.260 --> 00:03:46.480
Now you are ready to start
defining your own services

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:48.100
and setting SLOs against them.

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:50.178
Thanks for watching, and
come back soon for more.

00:03:50.178 --> 00:03:51.220
This is The Stack Doctor.

00:03:51.220 --> 00:03:52.210
Stay healthy out there.

00:03:52.210 --> 00:03:55.560
[MUSIC PLAYING]

